CL injury/insufficiency ---**2 VARUS STRESS TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the lateral collateral ligament LCL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is slightly flexed approximately 20 30 • Stabilize the medial aspect of the knee • Apply a varus force to the lower leg POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive lateral joint opening • Pain along the lateral knee INDICATES LCL injury/insufficiency ---**B CRUCIATE LIGAMENTS** ---**3 LACHMAN TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament ACL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee flexed approximately 20 30 • Stabilize the femur with one hand • Hold the proximal tibia with the other hand • Pull the tibia anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior translation of tibia • Soft/absent firm end feel compared with the normal side INDICATES ACL injury/tear Very important Lachman is generally more sensitive for ACL injury than the anterior drawer test particularly in the acute knee ---**4 ANTERIOR DRAWER TEST** PURPOSE To assess the ACL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Hip flexed and knee flexed to 90 • Stabilize the foot • Grasp the proximal tibia with both hands • Pull the tibia anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior movement of the tibia compared with the opposite side INDICATES ACL injury/insufficiency ---**5 POSTERIOR DRAWER TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the posterior cruciate ligament PCL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee flexed to approximately 90 • Stabilize the foot • Place both hands around the proximal tibia • Push the tibia posteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive posterior translation of the tibia INDICATES PCL injury/insufficiency ---**II TESTS FOR MENISCAL INJURY** ---**6 MCMURRAYS TEST** PURPOSE To assess for medial and lateral meniscal lesions PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Fully flex the knee • For medial meniscus externally rotate the tibia and apply valgus stress while extending the knee • For lateral meniscus internally rotate the tibia and apply varus stress while extending the knee • Palpate the joint line POSITIVE SIGN • Pain • Palpable/audible click or clunk at the joint line INDICATES Possible meniscal tear ---**7 APLEYS COMPRESSION DISTRACTION TEST** PURPOSE To differentiate meniscal pathology from ligament pathology PROCEDURE Compression • Patient lies prone • Knee flexed to 90 • Stabilize the thigh • Apply downward compression through the heel • Rotate the tibia internally and externally Distraction • Maintain the same position • Apply traction to the tibia while rotating it POSITIVE SIGN Compression positive • Pain reproduced with compression/rotation Suggests meniscal pathology Distraction positive • Pain reproduced with traction/rotation Suggests ligament/capsular pathology ---**III JOINT EFFUSION PATELLOFEMORAL DYSFUNCTION** ---**8 FLUCTUATION TEST** PURPOSE To detect knee joint effusion particularly larger effusions PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the knee relaxed/extended • Examiner compresses the fluid from one side of the knee toward the opposite side • Observe/palpate for a fluid wave POSITIVE SIGN • A palpable/visible fluid wave or fluctuation INDICATES Knee joint effusion ---**9 PATELLAR TAP TEST** PURPOSE To detect knee joint effusion PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with knee extended • Gently press/compress the suprapatellar region to move fluid beneath the patella • Tap the patella downward against the femur POSITIVE SIGN • Patella rebounds/floats and taps the femur producing a palpable tap INDICATES Significant knee joint effusion ---**10 CLARKES TEST / PATELLAR GRIND TEST** PURPOSE To assess patellofemoral dysfunction particularly chondromalacia/patellofemoral pain PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the knee extended • Examiner places the hand proximal to the patella • Apply gentle downward pressure to the patella • Ask the patient to contract the quadriceps POSITIVE SIGN • Pain behind/around the patella • Inability to maintain quadriceps contraction because of pain INDICATES Possible patellofemoral dysfunction/chondral pathology ---**IV OTHER MEASUREMENT** ---**11 Q ANGLE** PURPOSE To assess patellofemoral alignment and the lateral pull on the patella MEASUREMENT Q angle is formed by the intersection of • Line 1 ASIS centre of patella • Line 2 centre of patella tibial tubercle PROCEDURE • Patient positioned with the knee in the appropriate standardized position • Identify and mark • ASIS • Centre of patella • Tibial tubercle • Use a goniometer to measure the angle between the two lines POSITIVE/ABNORMAL FINDING An increased Q angle may indicate increased lateral patellar alignment/pull and may be associated with patellofemoral dysfunction INDICATES • Patellofemoral malalignment • Predisposition to lateral patellar tracking/instability\n\n--------------------\n\n10 Special Test: Ankle & Foot Complex:\nTests for Ligament Stability: Anterior Drawer Test, Talar Tilt Test etc.\nTests for Vascular Conditions (e.g., DVT): Homan's Test, Moses Test etc.\nPFDP 10 ankle foot special tests.pdf\n---**I TESTS FOR LIGAMENT STABILITY** ---**1 ANTERIOR DRAWER TEST ANKLE** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the anterior talofibular ligament ATFL and lateral ankle stability PROCEDURE • Patient sits or lies supine • Keep the ankle in a neutral or slight plantar flexed position • Stabilize the distal tibia with one hand • Grasp the heel/talus with the other hand • Draw the foot/talus anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior translation compared with the opposite ankle • Soft/absent end feel • Pain may be reproduced INDICATES ATFL injury/lateral ankle instability ---**2 TALAR TILT TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the lateral ankle ligaments particularly the calcaneofibular ligament CFL PROCEDURE • Patient sits or lies supine • Stabilize the distal leg • Grasp the calcaneus/heel • Tilt the calcaneus into inversion POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive inversion compared with the opposite side • Pain or a soft end feel INDICATES CFL injury/lateral ankle instability ---**II TESTS FOR VASCULAR CONDITIONS DVT** ---**3 HOMANS TEST** PURPOSE Traditionally used to assess for deep vein thrombosis DVT of the lower limb PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is extended • Examiner supports the leg • Passively dorsiflex the ankle POSITIVE SIGN • Pain in the calf during passive ankle dorsiflexion INDICATES Traditionally considered suggestive of DVT ---**4 MOSES TEST** PURPOSE Traditionally used to assess for DVT PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the leg relaxed • Compress the calf anteroposteriorly • Then compress the calf laterally POSITIVE SIGN • Pain produced by anteroposterior compression but not by lateral compression INDICATES Traditionally considered suggestive of DVT","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now honing my tool selection process based on two critical instructions. First, I recall the rules for avoiding unnecessary tool calls (cat, ls, grep, etc.). Second, I'm making a conscious effort to explicitly list all relevant tools before executing any of them. The format is being applied, I believe, as I get used to it.\n\n\n**Clarifying Agent Assignments**\n\nI'm now clarifying how agents should handle multiple PDFs. The instructions emphasize separate lines for each PDF's topics. I understand now: each PDF's topic list needs a newline separator from other PDFs, but within a PDF, the continuous topic format needs no extra newlines. Also, I've received an image with a specific list that will be used.\n\n\n**Listing Shoulder Tests**\n\nI'm cataloging shoulder tests, starting with impingement (Hawkins-Kennedy, Neer's), labral lesions (O'Brien, KIM), scapular dys