r procedures, positive signs (pain, heaviness, instability), and indications (lumbar segmental instability, hamstring tightness). I'm also including tests for neurological dysfunction. Next I'll include tests for joint dysfunction and PFDP.\n\n\n**Adding Hip Tests**\n\nI'm now integrating the Trendelenburg Test to assess hip abductor function, noting the procedure (single leg stance), positive sign (pelvic drop), and indication (weakness in the weight-bearing gluteus medius). I've also detailed the Straight Leg Raising Test for neurological dysfunction, including the procedure and positive signs related to lumbosacral nerve root irritation. I'm focusing on the full range of motion.\n\n\n**Adding Slump Test**\n\nI'm now integrating the Slump Test into the system for assessing lumbo-sacral nerve root involvement, specifically detailing the procedure (sitting position, spinal flexion, knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion), positive signs (reproduction of familiar neural symptoms), and indications (lumbar radiculopathy). I am also adding the Schober's Test for lumbar spine flexion/mobility to the inventory.\n\n\n**Adding Hip Tests**\n\nI'm now integrating the FABER/Patrick's test, detailing the procedure, positive signs (pain indicating hip or SI joint pathology), and indications (hip or SI joint dysfunction). Also, I'm integrating the Gaenslen's Test, noting that pain in the sacroiliac region is a positive sign for SI joint dysfunction, including its procedure and indications.\n\n\n**Adding Hip Tests**\n\nI'm now integrating the Gillet's Test and March Test for sacroiliac joint mobility, outlining the procedures, positive signs (restricted/asymmetrical PSIS movement), and indications (hypomobility/dysfunction). I am also adding hip joint tests, focusing on hip pathology (Hip Scour, Leg Roll, Patrick's), labral lesions (Anterior Labral Tear), pediatric hip conditions (Barlow's, Galeazzi, Ortolani), and muscle dysfunction (Hip Lag, 90-90 SLR, Thomas, Nelaton's).\n\n\n**Int